    <span class="eyebrow">A PRACTICAL GUIDE FOR FIRST-TIME JOB SEEKERS</span>
    <h1>Find a role that fits—and apply safely</h1>
    <p>Searching for your first job can feel overwhelming. A simple routine helps you focus on suitable openings, prepare a stronger application and avoid wasting time on outdated or misleading posts.</p>

    <h2>1. Check the requirements before applying</h2>
    <p>Read the full eligibility details, not just the job title. Compare your degree, graduation year, experience, skills and location with the employer’s requirements. Note the closing date and any age, document or examination conditions. If an important detail is unclear, confirm it on the employer’s official recruitment page before sharing personal information.</p>

    <h2>2. Make your resume relevant and truthful</h2>
    <p>Keep your resume easy to scan and focused on the role. Put your education, relevant skills and strongest projects near the top. For each project, explain what you built, which tools you used and what you contributed. Include internships, volunteering or coursework when they demonstrate useful skills. Use keywords from the genuine job requirements where they accurately describe your experience; never claim skills or qualifications you do not have. Check your phone number, email address and file before submitting.</p>
    <p><a asp-controller="Resume" asp-action="Index">Use the free resume builder</a> to prepare a clear starting draft.</p>

    <h2>3. Apply through a source you can verify</h2>
    <p>Open the employer’s official careers website and make sure the role and location match the listing. Follow the employer’s application instructions, upload only requested documents and save a copy of your application confirmation. D2DJobs shares openings to help you discover opportunities; the employer controls hiring and final eligibility decisions.</p>

    <h2>4. Keep track and follow up thoughtfully</h2>
    <p>Record the company, role, application date, closing date and next step in a simple spreadsheet or notebook. Save promising jobs in your browser and revisit the employer’s site before applying, because openings can close or change. If you contact a recruiter, use the contact details published on the company’s official website and send one concise, polite message.</p>

    <h2>5. Watch for recruitment scams</h2>
    <p>Be cautious of anyone promising a guaranteed job, asking for payment to secure an interview, or requesting bank passwords, one-time passwords or unnecessary identity documents. Do not pay a recruiter to obtain a job offer. Verify suspicious messages independently with the employer, and do not rely only on a logo, social media post or email display name.</p>

    <h2>6. Prepare for the next step</h2>
    <p>For an interview, review the job description, learn what the company does, prepare examples of your work and test your internet connection if the meeting is online. For a walk-in, confirm the date, venue, documents to bring and whether registration is required. Give yourself enough travel time and keep copies of requested documents secure.</p>

